// ★★ THE PREDICATE — the same four computed results, a different question asked of them,
//                     and the one surviving answer collected TWICE.
//
// `02-the-desk.rho` selected by SHAPE: the pattern required the body to be an application of
// the outer binder, which admits Church numerals and refuses Church booleans.
//
// This program keeps the same pool and asks a SEMANTIC question instead. The pattern binds the
// whole computed term; the `where` clause is a predicate the substrate decides. It then asks
// that SAME question a second time on a different channel — the provenance wire — so the value
// arrives by two independent routes and the two answers can be compared.
//
//
// THE FOUR INPUTS, AND WHAT EACH REDUCES TO
// ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Each was also run on its own to confirm the reduction (`rholang` on the bare term):
//
//   #   source                              is              reduces to             β steps
//   ──  ──────────────────────────────────  ──────────────  ─────────────────────  ───────
//   1   ((λm.λn.λf.λx.(m f)((n f) x)) 2) 3  plus 2 3        ⟦λ.λ.(1(1(1(1(1 0)))))⟧      6
//                                                            = Church numeral 5
//   2   ((λm.λn.λf. m (n f)) 2) 3           mult 2 3        ⟦λ.λ.(1(1(1(1(1(1 0))))))⟧   7
//                                                            = Church numeral 6
//   3   (λb. b FALSE TRUE) TRUE             not true        ⟦λ.λ.0⟧  = FALSE             3
//   4   ((λp.λq. (p q) p) TRUE) TRUE        and true true   ⟦λ.λ.1⟧  = TRUE              4
//
//
// HOW #3 DERIVES — the one the predicate accepts
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//   NOT ≡ λb. b FALSE TRUE          TRUE ≡ λt.λf. t          FALSE ≡ λt.λf. f
//
//       (λb. b FALSE TRUE) TRUE
//   ⟶β  TRUE FALSE TRUE                 substitute TRUE for b
//    =  (λt.λf. t) FALSE TRUE
//   ⟶β  (λf. FALSE) TRUE                TRUE selects its FIRST argument
//   ⟶β  FALSE
//
//   Three β steps — matching the `^fired` ledger when this term is run alone.
//
//
// READING THE OUTPUT — de Bruijn indices, 0 = INNERMOST binder
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   λt.λf. f   body names the INNER binder  → index 0 → ⟦λ.λ.0⟧ = FALSE
//   λt.λf. t   body names the OUTER binder, one binder out
//                                          → index 1 → ⟦λ.λ.1⟧ = TRUE
//
//   The two Church booleans are distinguished ONLY by which binder the body names. Same arity,
//   same structure, one index apart.
//
//
// WHY THE FILTER IS DOING REAL WORK
// ─────────────────────────────────
// The guard asks `== lam t. lam f. f`, i.e. FALSE. Of the four computed results exactly one
// satisfies it:
//
//   #1 Church 5  REFUSED — wrong shape entirely (body is an application, not a variable)
//   #2 Church 6  REFUSED — likewise
//   #3 FALSE     ★ ACCEPTED — published to @"OUT", once per route
//   #4 TRUE      REFUSED — ⟦λ.λ.1⟧ vs ⟦λ.λ.0⟧: ONE INDEX APART
//
// #4 is the sharpest rejection in the set. It has the same arity and the same structure as the
// accepted term and differs only in which binder its body names — and the decision is made on
// the COMPUTED value, since nothing in this file spells either boolean's normal form.
//
// The refused three are not consumed and not destroyed; they are still resting on @"results"
// when the program comes to rest. Change the predicate to `lam t. lam f. t` and #4 settles
// instead — same pool, same computation, different answer.
//
//
// ★★ TWO ROUTES TO THE SAME VALUE — the reply channel and the provenance wire
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A served `[*]` publishes each computed branch TWICE — two separate data, deposited on two
// different channels, which answer two different questions:
//
//   channel           datum                the question it answers
//   ────────────────  ───────────────────  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   @"results"        the bare term        "what did the program compute?"
//   @"^spec-success"  [trace, [term…]]     "which branch computed it, and how did it get
//                                          there?"
//
// This file reads BOTH, under the SAME predicate, and publishes what each yields. The two
// observations on @"OUT" are the same value — and that agreement is *computed*, not asserted
// here: the terms are equal by the substrate's own `==`, which is how it was checked.
//
// ★ WHY THE SECOND RECEIVE STILL FINDS ITS MATCH — it is not competing for the first one's
// datum. The two receives consume from two different channels, and the branch was published to
// both, so the first receive taking the bare term off @"results" leaves the provenance pair on
// @"^spec-success" untouched. Neither route can starve the other; delete either one and the
// remaining one behaves identically.
//
// ★ …AND WHY THE SECOND RECEIVE NAMES NO POOL. @"^spec-success" is ONE channel for the whole
// run, fixed by the runtime, not a per-request channel derived from the `[*]`'s subject. There
// is no mapping from it back to @"results", and none is needed here: every `[*]` in this file
// targets @"results", so every datum resting on the provenance wire came from this pool.
//
// ⚠ That correspondence is CONTEXTUAL, not encoded — the datum carries a trace and the terms,
// and nothing that names the channel the request replied on. A program that speculated onto two
// different reply channels would see both sets of branches interleaved on @"^spec-success" with
// no field to tell them apart, and would have to discriminate on the terms themselves, as the
// guard below does. A `[*]` whose reply channel is read by nobody still publishes here.
//
// ⚠ The provenance datum is a two-element PAIR, not a flat list. The trace is one `EList` of
// step digests; the branch's terms are their OWN `EList` nested beside it. The pattern
//
//     @[trace, [term]]
//
// says exactly that, and the inner list is written as a singleton because λ-calculus is
// CONFLUENT: every path of a λ program reaches the same normal form, so a branch projects
// exactly one term. All four branches present that shape — swap the guard for any of the other
// three computed values and that branch is the one that settles — so the guard, not the
// pattern's arity, is what selects.
//
// The body then rebuilds the FIPS `success` ENTRY from the pair. The FIPS's rule is that an
// extra list is *concatenated to the end of the trace*, and its own reader takes the leaf as
// the entry's last element:
//
//     entry = trace.concat([term])          the FIPS entry, built here from the wire pair
//     leaf  = entry.nth(entry.length() - 1) the FIPS reader's own projection
//
// So the trace route's value travels through a list construction, a concatenation, a length
// and an index before it lands — and still lands on the term the reply channel delivered
// directly. `.nth` is TOTAL: an out-of-range index is a recoverable `error` term, so a wrong
// index would fail loudly here rather than quietly print something plausible.
//
// ⚠⚠ A `where` guard MUST NOT contain a method call. `terms.nth(0) == lambda`lam t. lam f. f``
// looks like the natural way to write this predicate and is the one to avoid: a lowered guard
// is decided by `rho-pure-eval` inside the rspace matcher, which answers
// `UnsupportedExpression { kind: "EMethodBody" }` for ANY method, and the matcher commits only
// on `GBool(true)`. The receive therefore NEVER fires, the datum just rests, and the program
// still exits 0 — a filter that looks like it works and admits nothing. Bind what the guard
// needs in the PATTERN, as above, and compare it directly.
//
//
// WHAT THE COUNTERS DO
// ────────────────────
// `^spec-success: N` counts the provenance data still RESTING at the end, not the number of
// branches explored. Four branches are published and this program consumes one, so it reports
// 3 where beats 1, 2 and 4 report every branch they published. There is no peek in this
// grammar — `<-` and `<=` are the only receive arrows — so reading provenance necessarily
// consumes it.
//
//
// HOW TO RUN
// ──────────
//   target/debug/rholang demos/flt-lookahead/03-predicate.rho
//
// EXPECT: @"OUT" carries ⟦λ.λ.0⟧ TWICE — once from the reply channel, once rebuilt from the
// trace — and the run reports `lookahead: 4 request(s) served`, `^spec-success: 3`,
// `^spec-failure: 0`, `^spec-truncated: 0`.

@"results"!(lambda`((lam m. lam n. lam f. lam x. ((m, f), ((n, f), x)), lam f. lam x. (f, (f, x))), lam f. lam x. (f, (f, (f, x))))`)[*] |
@"results"!(lambda`((lam m. lam n. lam f. (m, (n, f)), lam f. lam x. (f, (f, x))), lam f. lam x. (f, (f, (f, x))))`)[*] |
@"results"!(lambda`(lam b. ((b, lam t. lam f. f), lam t. lam f. t), lam t. lam f. t)`)[*] |
@"results"!(lambda`((lam p. lam q. ((p, q), p), lam t. lam f. t), lam t. lam f. t)`)[*] |

for(@lambda`${r}` <- @"results" where lambda`${r}` == lambda`lam t. lam f. f`) {
  @"OUT"!(r)
} |

for(@[trace, [term]] <- @"^spec-success" where term == lambda`lam t. lam f. f`) {
  @"OUT"!(trace.concat([term]).nth(trace.concat([term]).length() - 1))
}
